description:
FILED s514f (Opus, 2026-10-11) from the #3144 board acceptance.  Exposed by #3144 (before it, TieLC's constructor died); the mechanism is #2590's (s514d).

A tied handle whose OBJECT is itself a handle opened with `>&=STDOUT` (fdopen of the SAME fd) recurses without bound: PCL keys the tied-handle side table by the STREAM the glob's IO slot holds (ir-spec §2.4b, tie-aggregates §7), and `open $self, ">&=STDOUT"` gives the object's glob the very stream STDOUT holds -- so `print {$self} @_` inside PRINT finds STDOUT's tie again and calls PRINT again (binding-stack exhaustion; on the board: Capture-Tiny t/13-stdout-tied.t and t/14-stderr-tied.t TIMEOUT, rc=124, where perl passes).

Minimal shape (Capture-Tiny t/lib/TieLC.pm):
    package TieLC;
    sub TIEHANDLE { my $c = shift; my $fh = \do { local *HANDLE }; bless $fh, $c; open($fh, $_[0]) or die; $fh }
    sub PRINT { my $self = shift; print {$self} @_ }
    package main;
    tie *STDOUT, 'TieLC', ">&=STDOUT";
    print "hello\n";   untie *STDOUT;
perl 5.40.3: hello.   PCL: unbounded recursion (probe with a depth guard).

perl's tie lives on the GLOB's IO (the IO object of *STDOUT), not on the fd/stream; a `>&=` handle is a distinct IO sharing the fd.  Fix direction: key the side table by the IO slot / glob identity, or give a `>&=` / `>&` open its own stream wrapper object so two IOs never share the key.  Measure the fhprint/fhread count test again (s514d's step 0) before changing the key.


MEASURED (s514f, two board runs of the same tree): run 1 13/14-*-tied.t TIMEOUT rc=124; run 2 both PASS (1 of 1 row, rc 0).  Run alone (tools/run-dist-t.pl), the TAP `ok 1` / `1..1` is complete and the recursion (binding-stack exhaustion, ~1000 frames of %p-tied-call-value -> TieLC::PRINT) hits AFTER it -- so the verdict depends on whether the stack blowup ends the process before the harness timeout.  The row count is right; the process is not.
